WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.700
The moons of Mars explained.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.360
Mars has two moons, Phobos and Deimos.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.180
They are really tiny. How tiny?

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.660
Compared to Mars or our own moon,

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.660
pretty tiny.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.280
Although, tiny is a matter of opinion.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.340
Their surface area is up close to some of the smallest states on Earth,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.020
like Luxembourg and Malta.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.000
Although Phobos and Deimos are in no way lightweight,

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.933
In reality their gravitational pull isn't even strong enough to bring them into spherical form.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.320
So they look more like huge potatoes than moons.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.120
The most popular theory of their origin is that they were once part of the asteroid belt

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.540
until Jupiter's massive gravity kicked them out of it.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.800
So Mars could catch them.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:56.180
Phobos orbits Mars at a average distance of 9,400 kilometers, once every 7 and a half hours.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.820
It's on a collision course, and gets 2 meters closer to Mars every year.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.420
In 50 to 100 million years, it will be either ripped to pieces by Mars' gravity,

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.280
and be transformed into a beautiful ring,

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.780
or it will crash into Mars.

00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:17.073
The energy released in this collision would kill everything on the small planet.

00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:22.075
So, if there are humans on Mars by then, they should build very strong bunkers.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.640
Smaller Deimos, on the other hand, is slowly escaping Mars.

00:01:26.733 --> 00:01:31.773
Eventually, it will fly off into space and leave a lonely red planet behind.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.580
So, in a few hundred million years, Mars will be moonless and on it's own.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.420
Unless, it manages to catch itself another asteroid.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:49.900
English subtitles by Dan9er

